# RPC server policies

Build the service contract first, then decide how the application discovers it and which cross-cutting policies wrap dispatch. The RPC builder methods come from <Symbol path="upwell_rpc::plugin::RpcAppBuilder" /> and are available through the daemon prelude.

## Register services deliberately

```toml
[dependencies]
upwell = { version = "1", features = ["daemon"] }
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
tokio = { version = "1", features = ["macros", "rt-multi-thread", "net"] }
```

<Example title="Choose one registration mode">

```rust
use upwell::daemon::prelude::*;

let discovered = App::builder("catalog-rpc")
    .auto_discover()
    .build()
    .await?;
```

Automatic discovery merges link-time service descriptors and their component factories. Use it when every linked service for this binary belongs on the endpoint.

```rust
let explicit = App::builder("catalog-rpc")
    .service::<CatalogService>()
    .build()
    .await?;
```

`service::<T>()` registers the service descriptor and constructs `T` from its component descriptor. `with_service(value)` instead installs a pre-built singleton and its service identity. `service_descriptor` is the lower-level raw-descriptor path.

</Example>

Preparation rejects duplicate service IDs, duplicate RPC names or paths, and empty services before accepting a transport.

## Order middleware and guards

<Example title="Wrap dispatch with an outer trace and an auth guard">

```rust
let app = App::builder("catalog-rpc")
    .service::<CatalogService>()
    .middleware(trace_layer)
    .guard(auth_guard)
    .error_handler(error_handler)
    .rpc_limits(RpcLimits::new(1024, 256))
    .build()
    .await?;
```

The first registered Tower layer is outermost. A guard is adapted into a layer that checks <Symbol path="upwell_rpc::descriptors::RpcCallContext" /> before calling the inner service. Therefore registration order determines whether a custom layer observes guard rejection and whether it runs before or after authentication.

</Example>

The terminal router builds a request scope and dispatches the matched generated handler. Layer `Service::ready` backpressure is honored. A guard returning `Err(ErrorResponse)` short-circuits the handler but still produces an outgoing RPC error response.

## Map outgoing failures globally

<Symbol path="upwell_rpc::middleware::ErrorHandler" /> receives the path and every outgoing <Symbol path="upwell_rpc::extract::ErrorResponse" />. It can log, enrich, or remap the response. There is one global handler.

The handler sees:

- a guard rejection;
- a route or extraction/DI failure;
- a typed application error converted through `ResponseError`;
- a response-stream error;
- an internal response synthesized after request-scope, handler, stream, or error-handler panic.

It does not turn startup, listener accept, connection-scope construction, or transport write failures into application error bodies. Those are outside the call-response boundary. If the global error handler panics, the server substitutes a sanitized internal error.

## Configure actual `RpcLimits`

<Symbol path="upwell_rpc::protocol::RpcLimits" /> controls only:

| Setting | Effect |
|---|---|
| `max_connections` | stops calling `accept` while the live connection task set is full |
| `max_calls_per_connection` | closes a connection when a new inbound call would exceed its in-flight task limit |
| `with_accept_backoff(initial, maximum)` | exponential retry delay for transient listener/resource-exhaustion accept errors |

Defaults in the validated inventory are 1024 connections and 256 in-flight calls per connection. Both constructor limits must be nonzero; the backoff initial duration must be nonzero and the maximum must not be smaller.

<Callout type="warning" title="Admission is not a complete resource policy">

`RpcLimits` does not set payload bytes, frame bytes, stream item count, handler timeout, or a global call total. Configure framing with the transport's frame configuration, enforce application timeouts in middleware or handlers, and bound streams in their own contracts.

</Callout>

## Failure and cancellation boundaries

Each accepted connection gets a connection scope. Each call gets a request scope, a cancellation token, and its own task. When a connection closes or server shutdown arrives, the call table is dropped and in-flight call tokens are cancelled; handler tasks still winding down are aborted.

A transient `accept` error backs off while continuing to observe shutdown. A terminal accept error stops admission, cancels connection loops, waits for tasks, and returns an error from `serve`. A connection-scope build failure closes only that connection. A request-scope build failure returns an error to that call when possible.

## Troubleshoot server policy

- A guard running before expected tracing usually means the tracing layer was registered inside it; remember first middleware is outermost.
- A custom application error bypassing the global handler likely never became `ErrorResponse`; implement the expected response conversion.
- Connections closing at load while global capacity remains can indicate the per-connection call limit.
- Large payload failures are not configured through `RpcLimits`; inspect transport frame limits.
- A duplicate route error is a preparation failure and cannot be repaired by an error handler.

